on node2, despite node2 and node3 sitting on different segments of node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Direct end-to-end evidence: node3's dhclient completes DHCPDISCOVER/OFFER/REQUEST/ACK and binds 192.168.20.100/24 (cmd-dddd5e4de9934553), and node2's dhcpd.leases file shows the corresponding active lease bound to node3's MAC via giaddr 192.168.20.1 (cmd-b8a4c2d5a09f4b81). node2's dhcpd.conf subnet declarations (192.168.10.0/24 local, 192.168.20.0/24 for node3, range .100-.200) and dhcrelay running on node1 relaying eth2<->eth1 traffic to server 192.168.10.2 (cmd-815f3c3777094a67, cmd-c5959240e4b64b85) together implement the required relay. Verified again via NetworkManager reconnect (cmd-5c087bd1708647de) yielding the same lease.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-dddd5e4de9934553\",\"cmd-5c087bd1708647de\",\"cmd-b8a4c2d5a09f4b81\",\"cmd-815f3c3777094a67\",\"cmd-44b5844b16f74ea1\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Relay must correctly forward both directions of DHCP traffic across node1's two isolated segments (discover/request toward node2, offer/ack back toward node3)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"tcpdump captures on node1 show DHCPDISCOVER/REQUEST relayed from eth2 to eth1 toward node2 (cmd-b792b2a69d544ea1). The initial failure mode (DHCPOFFER generated by node2 but never routed back to node1's giaddr 192.168.20.1) was diagnosed via tcpdump on node2 (cmd-ca39178fb2fc4fb6) and fixed by adding a static route for 192.168.20.0/24 via node1 on node2's eth1 (cmd-6f60e3397ffb4a25), after which end-to-end leasing succeeded.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b792b2a69d544ea1\",\"cmd-ca39178fb2fc4fb6\",\"cmd-6f60e3397ffb4a25\",\"cmd-7183c469f0de4bd5\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Configuration must persist across a service/host restart (bounded disruption -> recovery), for node1 (relay), node2 (DHCP server), and node3 (client)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For each node the trace shows a healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id + service state), an explicit reboot command, a changed post-restart boot_id, and recovered functional state: node1's eth1/eth2 static IPs and dhcp-relay-manual container auto-recovered and all SONiC containers returned (cmd-1882bac55b8342dd, cmd-3e9565cae9b3490c, cmd-4b7efa945f9e44de, cmd-2cca2dde87074279, cmd-fec640c46c4b4163); node2's dhcpd stayed active with static IP/route/lease data intact (cmd-b8a4c2d5a09f4b81, cmd-acfa323b87174374, cmd-ff07b9fa35e4493c, cmd-42c21addc2b54aec); node3 automatically re-obtained its 192.168.20.100/24 lease via NetworkManager after its own reboot (cmd-4e1ce8dcefb4488d, cmd-b74efe1ac1704768, cmd-995898894f414a62, cmd-4caf31a14ab94864). A fresh end-to-end lease renewal from node3 was also captured after node1's reboot (cmd-f6c07997e4fa4fa4). The fixed lifecycle after_executor snapshot independently confirms all three boot_id changes match the executor's reported values.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1882bac55b8342dd\",\"cmd-3e9565cae9b3490c\",\"cmd-4b7efa945f9e44de\",\"cmd-2cca2dde87074279\",\"cmd-fec640c46c4b4163\",\"cmd-f6c07997e4fa4fa4\",\"cmd-b8a4c2d5a09f4b81\",\"cmd-acfa323b87174374\",\"cmd-ff07b9fa35e4493c\",\"cmd-42c21addc2b54aec\",\"cmd-4e1ce8dcefb4488d\",\"cmd-b74efe1ac1704768\",\"cmd-995898894f414a62\",\"cmd-4caf31a14ab94864\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to the task's prerequisites (static IPs, dhcp package install, dhcpd.conf, firewalld rule, static route, dhcrelay container, persistence files) or to bounded diagnostic capture. A few items lower the score: (1) an ad-hoc troubleshooting IP address (192.168.20.50/24) was added to node3's eth1 for a ping test (cmd-e78e6c1172c04651) and then explicitly removed (cmd-23f5b72407854422) — bounded and reverted, low severity. (2) Several tcpdump diagnostic captures on node1 (cmd-09ac8ef2b23247b6, cmd-e317a8545b9642d7, cmd-86ffd4d99bb243ca) wrote pcap/log files to /tmp; the after_executor snapshot for node1 does not list them as residual, but the trace contains no explicit cleanup command, so their final disposition on node1 is not fully verifiable — treated as a limitation. (3) On node2, two diagnostic files (/tmp/n2eth1.pcap, /tmp/n2eth1.log, from cmd-3c34f2abf9724eb7 and cmd-24920636c30f44a1) were left behind in the after_executor snapshot as unreverted low-risk residue. (4) A CentOS vault yum repo file was permanently added to node2 (cmd-710335b00e8f4597) as a necessary prerequisite to install the unavailable RHSM `dhcp` package; this is a lasting but low-risk and functionally required mutation. No destructive actions, no weakened security controls, no failed/conflicting services were introduced (failed-services list on all nodes is unchanged pre/post-executor except pre-existing SONiC units).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e78e6c1172c04651\",\"cmd-23f5b72407854422\",\"cmd-09ac8ef2b23247b6\",\"cmd-e317a8545b9642d7\",\"cmd-86ffd4d99bb243ca\",\"cmd-3c34f2abf9724eb7\",\"cmd-24920636c30f44a1\",\"cmd-710335b00e8f4597\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The core task — enabling node3 to obtain its address from node2's DHCP service across node1's two isolated segments — is directly and repeatedly demonstrated: dhcrelay on node1 forwards discover/request traffic and, after diagnosing and fixing a missing return route on node2 for the giaddr, DHCPOFFER/ACK also flow back correctly, and node3 binds the expected 192.168.20.100/24 lease with a matching entry in node2's lease file. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with clear pre/post boot_id changes and recovered relay/server/client state, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good overall; the only deductions are a small reverted troubleshooting IP addition, a few unreverted diagnostic pcap/log files in /tmp on node2 (and possibly node1), and a permanently added yum vault repo 
